  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/mountain_crystal_tetra_leptagoniates_pi_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Mountain crystal tetra attains a length of about 4 cm. Very characteristic and species-specific is the swim bladder, which is parted in two and has a connection on the top, forming a figure reminiscent of the ancient Greek letter \"Pi\". This is why the species is named as it is.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/mountain_crystal_tetra_leptagoniates_pi_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species was originally described from Bolivia. The fish imported by the source came from Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/mountain_crystal_tetra_leptagoniates_pi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Any usual fish food is readily taken.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/mountain_crystal_tetra_leptagoniates_pi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Mountain crystal tetras are very peaceful fishes, maybe a bit shy. They should always be kept in schools. Although they are so colourless (or maybe because of it) they are real eye-catchers. The species can be kept in community tanks without problems. Aquatic plants will not be damaged by the fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/mountain_crystal_tetra_leptagoniates_pi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Glassfishes occur in many fish families. In the hobby, glass perches (Parambassis etc.) and glass catfishes (Kryptopterus) are well known; occasionally glass gobies (Gobiopterus) and Crystal danios (Danionella) are also kept. All these come from South and South East Asia. The Mountain crystal tetra is a species from South America that is new to the hobby.</p>"
    }
  ],
